PLACES=[
# ---- The Battlefords and the North Saskatchewan ----
N('fort-battleford','Fort Battleford National Historic Site','Battleford','sk','fort','standing',52.72682,-108.29498,'site','open','walk','forts','1876–1924',
  'A North West Mounted Police post built in 1876 on the plateau where the Battle River meets the North Saskatchewan, when Battleford was capital of the North West Territories. In the 1885 rebellion about 500 people took shelter in it during the "siege" of Battleford; it was the base for the fighting at Cut Knife Hill and Fort Pitt, Poundmaker surrendered to General Middleton here on May 26, 1885, and eight Cree and Stoney men were hanged here. The police left in 1924.',
  'Several small wooden buildings inside a stockade on a diamond-shaped ground. Two buildings survive from 1876–1885: the Commanding Officer\'s Residence (Red River frame) and the Officers\' Quarters (log), both plaster-coated with wood-shingle roofs and original interior layouts and finishes; the rest of the old buildings, the bastions and the 1885 earthworks beside the stockade survive as footprints and buried remains (register). From the edge there are views across the Battle River to the site of the old Government House.',
  'The police post where Poundmaker surrendered in 1885, two of its first buildings still standing.',
  'Parks Canada, on Central Avenue at the edge of Battleford. Seasonal; when this was written the site was closed for the year and due to reopen in 2027, and the season\'s days and times were not given (Parks Canada hours page, read November 2026). Day admission $5.00 adults, youth free (Parks Canada fees page, 2025). Parking not checked.',
  [HP('Fort Battleford National Historic Site of Canada',7611),W('Fort Battleford'),L('Parks Canada: Fort Battleford hours','https://www.parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/sk/battleford/visit/heures-hours'),L('Museums Association of Saskatchewan: Fort Battleford','https://saskmuseums.org/places/fort-battleford-national-historic-site/')],
  brick='no',hours='Summer season; closed for the year in November 2026, reopening 2027 (days and times not given)'),
N('fort-pitt-sk','Fort Pitt (Fort Pitt Provincial Park)','Frenchman Butte','sk','fort','traces',53.57197,-109.79242,'site','public','walk','forts','1829–1890',
  'A Hudson\'s Bay Company provision post founded by Chief Factor John Rowand in the winter of 1829–30 on the North Saskatchewan River, to trade for bison hides, meat and pemmican with the Cree, Assiniboine and Blackfoot. A second post was built about 100 m southwest in 1873. Treaty No. 6 was signed here in 1876 and the Mounted Police set up a base the same year. In April 1885 Big Bear\'s followers burned most of the fort after the police withdrew to Battleford; the company rebuilt some buildings but gave the post up by 1890.',
  'A field by the river where the remains of both forts have been partly dug and laid out, so the outlines of all the buildings and the palisade can be seen, with one building of the second fort reconstructed, a federal cairn and plaques to Fort Pitt and Big Bear (register). Wikipedia adds a picnic area and access to the river. A farmer who worked the land after 1944 found shallow graves near the first fort and was buried by the 1954 cairn (Wikipedia).',
  'Walk the laid-out outlines of the fort where Treaty 6 was signed and which Big Bear\'s men burned.',
  'Fort Pitt Provincial Park, a provincial historic park and national historic site, about 5 km northeast of Hewitt Landing (register). Season, hours and parking not checked: the Sask Parks page is drawn by script and could not be read.',
  [HP('Fort Pitt National Historic Site of Canada',18987),W('Fort Pitt Provincial Park')],brick='?'),
N('frenchman-butte-battlefield','Frenchman Butte battlefield (rifle pits)','Frenchman Butte','sk','fort','traces',53.62722,-109.57583,'area','view','walk','forts','1885',
  'On May 28, 1885 Cree warriors under the war chief Wandering Spirit, dug in on a hillside along Little Red Deer Creek, fought General T. B. Strange\'s Alberta Field Force for several hours; both sides withdrew, Strange\'s men to wait for more troops and the Cree north towards Loon Lake. It was one of the last fights of the rebellion, and a national historic site since 1929.',
  'The register says warrior, Field Force and civilian pits are still visible on the 7.2-hectare site: boulder-built Cree warrior and civilian pits along the coulee behind a defensive mound of earth, and shallow, roughly dug Field Force rifle pits along the crest of the ridge opposite. Not checked on the ground.',
  'Rifle pits from an 1885 battle still in the grass.',
  'Who owns the ground and how the public reaches it could not be confirmed from a page opened when this was written; the pin marks the area from the Wikipedia coordinate. Look from the road unless you find a sign that says otherwise.',
  [HP('Frenchman Butte National Historic Site of Canada',14509),W("Battle of Frenchman's Butte")],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('steele-narrows-battlefield','Steele Narrows (the battle of Loon Lake)','Loon Lake','sk','fort','marker',54.0401,-109.31886,'site','public','walk','forts','1885',
  'The last fight of the North-West Resistance of 1885, and called the last battle fought on Canadian soil: Sam Steele\'s scouts of the Alberta Field Force caught up with Big Bear\'s Cree, who held hostages from Fort Pitt, at the narrows between Makwa Lake and Sanderson Bay. The register says at least four and perhaps 15 Cree died, among them Chief Seekaskootch while trying to arrange a truce; the Cree withdrew into the forest and General Middleton\'s pursuit gave up in the muskeg. The date is not agreed: the register, Wikipedia and Tourism Saskatchewan give June 3, 1885, the federal marker June 2.',
  'An 88-hectare park of forest, hills and muskeg on both sides of the narrows, little changed. White concrete pylons mark the ford, where Cree fell and where shots were exchanged; there are marked and unmarked graves, interpretive signs and the federal plaque (register, Wikipedia, HMdb). First Nations hold ceremonies here.',
  '',
  'Steele Narrows Provincial Park, on Grid 699 about 10 to 15 km west of Loon Lake (register; Tourism Saskatchewan). Interpretive signs, a picnic area and a boat launch; the park\'s phone is on the Tourism Saskatchewan listing. Treat the graves with respect. Season not checked.',
  [HP('Steele Narrows Provincial Park',3113),W('Steele Narrows Provincial Park'),L('Tourism Saskatchewan: Steele Narrows Provincial Park','https://tourismsaskatchewan.com/provincialpark/928/steele-narrows-provincial-park'),L('HMdb: Steele Narrows marker','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=315466')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',th=[54.03949,-109.31741],thn='Parking on Grid 699 by the park'),
# ---- Cypress Hills and Wood Mountain ----
N('fort-walsh','Fort Walsh National Historic Site','Merryflat','sk','fort','standing',49.57282,-109.8814,'site','open','drive','forts','1875–1883; rebuilt 1940s',
  'A North West Mounted Police fort built in 1875 by Inspector James Morrow Walsh in the Cypress Hills, after the Cypress Hills Massacre, to stop the whisky trade and police the border; it was the force\'s headquarters from 1878 to 1882 and dealt with the Lakota who came north under Sitting Bull after the Little Bighorn. Assiniboine chiefs signed their adhesion to Treaty 4 here in 1877. It closed in 1883, was stripped for other posts and burned in 1886. In the 1940s the RCMP built a horse ranch on the site, used until 1968 to breed horses for the force and the Musical Ride.',
  'Ten log buildings the RCMP put up in the mid-twentieth century to recall the old fort, on a hill among rolling grass and woods; the remains of the palisade and of 46 buildings of the 1870s are in the ground (register). Guided tours of the fort; the townsite, two cemeteries and the massacre site are self-guided, with trails along the ridge by Battle Creek and a visitor centre (Museums Association listing).',
  'The Mounted Police headquarters of the Sitting Bull years, in the Cypress Hills.',
  'Parks Canada, part of Cypress Hills Interprovincial Park, 55 km southwest of Maple Creek on Highway 271 (Museums Association listing). Fees: see Parks Canada. Snacks only, no concession.',
  [HP('Fort Walsh National Historic Site of Canada',6666),W('Fort Walsh'),MAS_WALSH],th=[49.57454,-109.87389],thn='Parking on Fort Walsh Road',brick='no',hours='May 15 to September 19 (2026): Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.; closed Sunday and Monday'),
N('cypress-hills-massacre-site','Cypress Hills Massacre site (Farwell\'s and Solomon\'s posts)','Merryflat','sk','fort','standing',49.54917,-109.86806,'area','open','walk','','1873',
  'On June 1, 1873 a party of American wolfers and traders attacked a Nakoda (Assiniboine) camp beside Farwell\'s and Solomon\'s whisky-trading posts on Battle Creek, in a dispute over stolen horses; thirteen or more Nakoda and one wolfer died. The killings hurried the sending west of the new North West Mounted Police. The Nakoda hold the place as where the spirits of those who died are tied.',
  'A broad valley floor of native grass beside a wooded coulee, about 2 km south of Fort Walsh, with reconstructions of the two trading posts that the RCMP built as a centennial project; Parks Canada dug parts of the post sites in 1972 (register). Self-guided (Museums Association listing).',
  '',
  'Parks Canada, reached through Fort Walsh National Historic Site; open in Fort Walsh\'s season (Parks Canada; Museums Association listing). This is a burial place for the Nakoda: keep to the paths.',
  [HP('Cypress Hills Massacre National Historic Site of Canada',9307),W('Cypress Hills Massacre'),L('Parks Canada: Cypress Hills Massacre National Historic Site','https://parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/sk/walsh/info/cypress-hills-massacre'),MAS_WALSH],
  brick='?',hours='With Fort Walsh: May 15 to September 19 (2026), Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.'),
N('wood-mountain-post','Wood Mountain Post Provincial Park','Wood Mountain','sk','fort','standing',49.31525,-106.37900,'site','public','drive','forts','1887–1918',
  'A North West Mounted Police post in the Wood Mountain Hills, set up to keep relations with the First Nations, patrol the border and do general police work. Wikipedia gives the post as running from 1887 to 1918 and also says an earlier post closed in 1883 and reopened in 1885; Major James Walsh negotiated here with Sitting Bull, whose Lakota stayed in the area for five years.',
  'Two buildings, a barracks and a kitchen and storehouse, reconstructed on their own sites, with the foundations of the other buildings outlined; digs found the cellars and trails (Wikipedia). A picnic area; the regional park next door has a campground.',
  '',
  'Sask Parks, a 5.5-hectare historic park on Highway 18 about 8 km south of the village of Wood Mountain (Wikipedia). Season and hours not checked: the park pages could not be read.',
  [W('Wood Mountain Post Provincial Park')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
# ---- Gravelbourg, Moose Jaw, Regina, Last Mountain Lake ----
N('gravelbourg-co-cathedral','Our Lady of the Assumption Co-Cathedral, bishop\'s residence and convent, Gravelbourg','Gravelbourg','sk','worship','standing',49.87239,-106.55731,'site','open','drive','','1917–1919',
  'The church buildings at the heart of Gravelbourg, founded in 1907 by Father Louis-Pierre Gravel as a centre of French-Canadian Catholic settlement, all by the architect J.-E. Fortin: the convent of the Sisters of Jesus and Mary (1917, two wings added 1926–27), the bishop\'s residence (1918) and the cathedral (1918–19, consecrated November 5, 1919). It was the cathedral of the Diocese of Gravelbourg from 1930 until the diocese was merged into Regina\'s in 1998, and is now a co-cathedral.',
  'The cathedral is tan Claybank brick trimmed with stone, Romanesque and Italian Renaissance, with twin towers capped by cupolas 53 m high. Inside, Monsignor Charles Maillard painted the whole interior with Old and New Testament scenes between 1921 and 1931; there are Rault stained-glass windows and fine woodwork (Wikipedia, register, Tourism Saskatchewan). The four-storey convent beside it holds schools, a college and the library, and its chapel is shown by the town museum in summer (Town of Gravelbourg).',
  'A painted prairie cathedral: every wall covered by one priest over ten years.',
  'At the south end of Main Street. Guided tours of the cathedral year-round by arrangement through the number on the Town of Gravelbourg page; the convent chapel is shown through the Gravelbourg & District Museum, June to August.',
  [HP('Gravelbourg Ecclesiastical Buildings National Historic Site of Canada',11188),W('Our Lady of Assumption Co-Cathedral'),W('Gravelbourg Ecclesiastical Buildings'),L('Town of Gravelbourg: the co-cathedral','https://gravelbourg.ca/visitors/heritage_walking_tour/co_cathedral.html'),L('Town of Gravelbourg: national historic site','https://gravelbourg.ca/visitors/national_historic_site.html'),L('Tourism Saskatchewan: the co-cathedral','https://tourismsaskatchewan.com/listings/2142/our-lady-of-the-assumption-co-cathedral-notre-dame-de-lassomption')],
  brick='no',hours='Cathedral: guided tours year-round by arrangement; convent chapel June to August through the town museum'),
N('moose-jaw-court-house','Moose Jaw Court House','Moose Jaw','sk','civic','standing',50.39375,-105.53703,'exact','view','drive','','1908–1909',
  'The first of the district court houses the new province of Saskatchewan built, designed by Darling and Pearson of Toronto and put up in 1908–09 by Smith Brothers and Wilson of Regina; the only Saskatchewan court house by that firm. Wikipedia calls it the oldest provincial court house in the province still in use. A national historic site since 1980.',
  'A square two-and-a-half-storey Beaux-Arts building on a raised corner lot inside a low brick and stone fence: red-brown pressed brick with pale Bedford stone trim, brick columns and quoined pilasters across the front, the provincial crest over the door, a dentilled cornice and parapet (register). Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'A working court house at 64 Ominica Street West. See it from the street.',
  [HP('Moose Jaw Court House National Historic Site of Canada',13178),W('Moose Jaw Court House')],brick='yes'),
N('government-house-regina','Government House, Regina','Regina','sk','landmark','standing',50.45403,-104.6477,'exact','open','drive','','1891',
  'Built in 1891 to plans from Thomas Fuller\'s federal office as the residence of the Lieutenant Governor of the North-West Territories, on the prairie west of Regina; from 1905 it housed Saskatchewan\'s lieutenant governors until 1945 (the register; Wikipedia says it was vacated in 1944). It returned to ceremonial use in 1984 and now holds the lieutenant governor\'s offices and a museum.',
  'A two-storey Italianate mansion of buff brick with limestone trim on a stone foundation, with a porte-cochère, a greenhouse of 1901, a 1921 sunporch, a 1928 ballroom and a large addition of 2005. Inside: an elliptical lightwell under the skylight, tiled fireplaces, "mahoganized" millwork, bevelled glass, an archway on carved columns and the wood staircase. Parts of the Edwardian estate gardens survive; the outbuildings are gone (register).',
  'The house the territorial governors lived in, open to walk through.',
  'Government House, 4607 Dewdney Avenue. Free; donations suggested. Visitor parking lot off Connaught Street; Regina Transit stops in front (governmenthousesk.ca).',
  [HP('Government House National Historic Site of Canada',7620),W('Government House (Saskatchewan)'),L('Government House: plan your visit','https://governmenthousesk.ca/visit/plan-your-visit/')],
  brick='no',hours='Victoria Day to Labour Day: daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; winter: Tuesday to Sunday 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('sask-legislative-building','Saskatchewan Legislative Building','Regina','sk','civic','standing',50.43246,-104.61515,'exact','open','drive','','1908–1912',
  'The seat of the province\'s legislature, built 1908–12 in the Beaux-Arts style to a design by Edward and William Sutherland Maxwell of Montreal; Earl Grey laid the cornerstone in 1909 and the Duke of Connaught opened it in 1912. Premier Walter Scott had Frederick Todd lay out the grounds from 1907, and the Wascana reservoir was enlarged into the lake in front. Building and grounds became a national historic site in 2004.',
  'Three storeys of buff limestone over steel, a long block crossed by a porticoed centre and a great dome on a colonnaded octagon, on the south shore of Wascana Lake among drives, paths, formal flower beds and woods (register). Free guided tours in English or French.',
  '',
  'Legislative Assembly of Saskatchewan, 2405 Legislative Drive. Free tours every 30 minutes in summer and every hour in winter; closed Good Friday, Christmas, Boxing Day and New Year\'s Day. Two-hour visitor parking; barrier-free Prince of Wales entrance west of the main doors (Legislative Assembly pages).',
  [HP('Saskatchewan Legislative Building and Grounds National Historic Site of Canada',13054),W('Saskatchewan Legislative Building'),L('Legislative Assembly: tours','https://www.legassembly.sk.ca/tours/'),L('Legislative Assembly: building access','https://www.legassembly.sk.ca/about/saskatchewans-legislative-building/legislative-building-access/')],
  brick='no',hours='Tours daily: summer every 30 min 9 a.m.–4:30 p.m. (last); winter hourly 9–12 and 1–5 p.m.'),
N('last-mountain-house','Last Mountain House Provincial Park','Craven','sk','fort','standing',50.77407,-104.87181,'site','public','walk','','1869–c.1873',
  'A Hudson\'s Bay Company outpost of Fort Qu\'Appelle built in 1869 on the east side of Last Mountain Lake to take the bison-robe and pemmican trade; the first season brought in about 1,000 robes, the second was a failure as the bison moved south. The last record of it is for 1872–73; it was burned soon after it was left (register, Wikipedia). Its master was a 21-year-old apprentice clerk, Isaac Cowie.',
  'A reconstruction of the post, based on archives and an almost complete dig of the site: buildings round three sides of a square in Red River frame, with no palisade, on prairie upland above the lake and a deep ravine, in a 59.8-hectare park (register).',
  '',
  'Sask Parks, a provincial historic park about 7 km northwest of Craven and 50 km north of Regina (register, Wikipedia). Season and hours not checked: the park pages could not be read.',
  [HP('Last Mountain House Provincial Park',3734),W('Last Mountain House Provincial Park')],brick='no'),
# ---- Humboldt, Prince Albert, the east and the north ----
N('humboldt-post-office','Humboldt Post Office (Humboldt & District Museum)','Humboldt','sk','civic','standing',52.19901,-105.12276,'exact','open','drive','','1911–1912',
  'A federal building put up in 1911–12 to plans signed by David Ewart of the Dominion Architect\'s office, holding the post office, customs and excise, the telegraph office and later the RCMP. The register and Wikipedia call it the last survivor of a series of near-identical buildings on the prairies. A national historic site since 1977; it now houses the Humboldt & District Museum and Gallery.',
  'Two and a half storeys of red-brown brick with buff limestone, a high mansard roof sheathed in silver metal with dormered gables, round-headed Romanesque windows with brick voussoirs on the ground floor, and a four-storey clock and bell tower on the corner over the double entrance (register).',
  '',
  'Humboldt & District Museum, 602 Main Street, at Main and 6th Avenue. Admission by donation; wheelchair accessible; closed on statutory holidays (humboldtmuseum.ca).',
  [HP('Humboldt Post Office National Historic Site of Canada',11807),W('Humboldt Post Office'),L('Humboldt Museum: hours','https://humboldtmuseum.ca/hours-of-operation/')],
  brick='yes',hours='July–Aug: Tue–Sat 10–12 and 1–5; Sept–June: Tue–Fri 1–5, Sat 10–12 and 1–5'),
N('keyhole-castle','Keyhole Castle','Prince Albert','sk','landmark','standing',53.19521,-105.75195,'exact','view','drive','','1913',
  'A Queen Anne Revival mansion finished in 1913 for Samuel McLeod, merchant, lumberman, mayor of Prince Albert and federal politician, to designs by the Minneapolis architect Erich Wojahn. It became a national historic site in 1975. The register calls it a private residence; Wikipedia says it is a bed and breakfast and event venue.',
  'Two and a half storeys of red brick with all the trim painted white, a red tile roof with scrolled gables, a conical corner tower with the keyhole-shaped dormer windows that give it its name, a porte-cochère and a sunroom and gallery. Inside the register lists exotic hardwoods, marble, gold leaf in the dining room, bevelled and stained glass and a small ballroom under the roof. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Privately owned, at 1925 1st Avenue East in the East Hill neighbourhood. See it from the street unless you are a guest.',
  [HP('Keyhole Castle National Historic Site of Canada',7627),W('Keyhole Castle')],conf='mixed',brick='yes'),
N('veregin-doukhobor-prayer-home','Doukhobor Prayer Home, Veregin (National Doukhobor Heritage Village)','Veregin','sk','worship','standing',51.5817,-102.0841,'site','open','drive','','1904–1931',
  'Veregin was founded in 1904 as the administrative, distribution and spiritual centre of the Doukhobors who came to Canada from Russia, and was headquarters of Peter V. Verigin\'s Christian Community of Universal Brotherhood from 1917 until 1931. The two-storey prayer home of 1917 was his residence and the community\'s spiritual and administrative centre. The settlement declined after 1931 and was revived as a heritage village in the 1980s; it became a national historic site in 2006.',
  'The richly decorated two-storey prayer home, now a museum (a Commons photograph shows it white-painted with fretwork verandas on both floors and a red brick chimney), with the open meeting ground round it. Also original to the site: the drive shed (1910–12), the grain elevator (about 1908) and the foundations of the old store; and buildings moved in: a Doukhobor house, bakery, bath house, granary, barn and blacksmith, a statue of Leo Tolstoy, and a museum building with Verigin\'s own "Rockaway" coach (register, Wikipedia). Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'National Doukhobor Heritage Village, Veregin. Off season by appointment only (Museums Association listing). Fees not checked.',
  [HP('Doukhobors at Veregin National Historic Site of Canada',12783),W('Doukhobors at Veregin'),L('Museums Association of Saskatchewan: National Doukhobor Heritage Village','https://saskmuseums.org/places/national-doukhobor-heritage-village/')],
  brick='some',hours='Mid-May to mid-September: daily 10 a.m. to 6 p.m.; off season by appointment'),
N('fort-livingstone','Fort Livingstone (Swan River Barracks)','Pelly','sk','fort','traces',51.90375,-101.95751,'area','view','walk','forts','1874–1884',
  'Built in 1874–75 by the Department of Public Works as the Swan River Barracks, the first post built for the North West Mounted Police and their first headquarters, and the first capital of the North-West Territories (the register gives 1876–77, Wikipedia 1874 to 1876). It housed 185 men. The capital and the police headquarters soon moved on, and in 1884 a prairie fire destroyed the fort.',
  'On a granite hill above Snake Creek near the Swan River, among grass and groves of aspen and hawthorn: the foundations and cellars of the married men\'s and men\'s quarters, the officers\' quarters, the hospital and three other buildings, and traces of the early telegraph line (register). Not checked on the ground.',
  '',
  'A national historic site and provincial protected area on Highway 661, about 16 km north of Pelly (register, Wikipedia). The pin is the OpenStreetMap point for the site. How the public reaches the remains was not confirmed: look from the road unless signs say otherwise.',
  [HP('Fort Livingstone National Historic Site of Canada',11599),W('Fort Livingstone (Saskatchewan)')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('cumberland-house-powder-magazine','Cumberland House: the 1886 powder magazine and the Northcote\'s remains','Cumberland House','sk','fort','standing',53.95999,-102.26214,'area','public','drive','','1774; 1886',
  'Cumberland House, founded in 1774, was the Hudson\'s Bay Company\'s first inland post in Saskatchewan and is the oldest village in the province, later a stop for the steamboats on the Saskatchewan River. The site has been a national historic site since 1924 and a provincial historic park since 1986.',
  'The remaining historic pieces are a powder magazine for storing explosives, built in 1886, and pieces of the company\'s steamboat Northcote (Wikipedia). The park is on Cumberland Island on the Bigstone River. Not checked on the ground.',
  '',
  'Cumberland House Provincial Park, in the village of Cumberland House. Season, hours and parking not checked.',
  [W('Cumberland House Provincial Park')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('holy-trinity-stanley-mission','Holy Trinity Anglican Church, Stanley Mission','Stanley Mission','sk','worship','standing',55.41757,-104.55069,'exact','boat','boat','','1854–1860',
  'Built between 1854 and 1860 on a rocky point on the Churchill River, designed by the missionary Rev. Robert Hunt and built mostly by local Cree with locally cut timber and with hardware, nails and coloured glass brought from England; the first service, in Cree, was held on June 10, 1860. It is the oldest standing building in Saskatchewan, and the register calls it the oldest church west of the Red River. The mission once had nearly 30 buildings around it.',
  'A large white wood-frame Gothic Revival church with a 23 m spire seen from far off against the forest. Inside, the register lists pointed arches between the tall nave and the aisles, an elaborate beamed vaulted ceiling, a clerestory and windows set with squares of coloured glass, and a narrower chancel. When it opens to visitors was not checked.',
  'The oldest building in Saskatchewan, reached only by boat.',
  'Run by Sask Parks as a provincial historic site, across the river from the community of Stanley Mission; reached by boat from Stanley Mission (Wikipedia). There is a cemetery by the church.',
  [HP('Holy Trinity Church National Historic Site of Canada',12545),W('Holy Trinity Anglican Church Provincial Historic Site')],brick='no'),
]
